For mpv 0.42.0. Not from upstream.

On an exact seek a little way forward, decode on from where playback is
and do not restart the decoder at a keyframe.

An exact seek resets the decoders, moves the demuxer to the keyframe
before the target and decodes from there to the target, dropping the
frames on the way. For a short jump forward that keyframe is usually
behind the current position: in a film with a keyframe every 10 seconds, a
jump of 3 seconds decodes 5 seconds of video on average and up to 10, all
but 3 of which the decoder had just decoded.

With this patch, such a seek leaves the video and audio decoders and the
demuxer where they are when there is no keyframe between the reader's
position and the target, the packets up to the target are queued, and the
target is at least 1 second ahead of the picture on screen. The decoders go
on with the next packet, exactly as plain playback would, and everything
else about the seek is unchanged: the outputs are reset, frames before the
target are dropped, and the audio syncs to the first video frame.

  filters/filter.c   mp_filter_set_reset_exempt(): a filter that
                     mp_filter_reset() leaves alone while it is set.
  demux/demux.c      demux_reads_on_to(): whether a stream's reader gets
                     to a pts through queued packets without a keyframe.
  player/playloop.c  can_decode_on() decides, mp_seek() skips the demuxer
                     seek, and the reset keeps the two decoders and the
                     subtitle decoders, which hold packets that would not
                     be read again.

Not changed: seeks backwards, to keyframes, by frame steps, during another
seek, across a keyframe, past what is buffered, in reverse playback, and
with external audio or subtitle files.

